{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1536721-gaian-s-interactions","id":1536721,"title":"Gaian's interactions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-06-02T17:15:03-07:00","id":1536721,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Gaian's interactions","content":"This is going to be one of those questions, what if the two world of darkness met. In CofD there are varieties of monsters that don't exist in the classic: Sin-eaters, Promethean, Beast, Divergents and there may be more. What would be the Garou nation's position regarding these species? If I were to guess it would be something like this:<br /> <br /> Sin-Eaters: Avoid whenever possible.<br /> Promethean: If you can, kill, if you can't, keep your distance.<br /> Beast: KILL, no questions asked.<br /> Divergent: Don't waste your time with them, but don't hesitate to kill if you feel necessary.<br /> <br /> But anyway, does anyone think differently? I'm just curious.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T14:25:25-07:00","id":1539052,"title":"","content":"Werewolves like to think of themselves as being Wyld aligned, and Wyrm opposed. Wyld represents life without death, creativity without meaning, chaos without order. I think that, other than Gryphon and Pegasus' tribes, the only reason most Garou are Wyld aligned is because, at the moment, in the sunlit realm, it seems to be losing, and is therefore <i>incrementally</i> less dangerous than its siblings. They also, in their dealings with various night-folk, seem to combine the human distaste for outsiders and strangers with the wolf distaste for anything alien, and so, yes, are quick to designate anything not obviously allied as a target of violence or scorn. Combined with a long ancestral memory and the ability to very quickly form grudges it makes sense that 'avoid' or 'kill' would be their go to for a lot of strange things.<br /> <br /> Still, I like to think that Beasts and Deviants could reek of Wyld enough more than they do of Devourer or Defiler Wyrm that they might get a pass, particularly from Red Talons if they are inimical to humans or human society. Like I often think the link between Luna and figures like Lilith or Tiamat or Cipactli or Coyoxauhqui was underplayed. But I also like giving my Garou some 'trailer-park-cult-to-the-beast-of-a-thousand-legs kindof flavor where I can.<br /> <br /> Though, the more I think of it, it seems like the average Cliath Garou would see even a relatively pure Deviant and think, 'Weird, that fomor doesn't reek of Wyrm and doesn't seem to be attended by any banes. Oh well.' Seems like you'd need a patient, perceptive or experienced Garou to get to the &quot;'don't waste your time'&quot; ending.<br /> <br /> But yeah, Prometheans are clearly corruptions of the natural order whether Wyrmish or Weaverish, I can't imagine the strong creative spark required to make them really changing a Garou's mind on that; and Sin-Eaters would have the same problems dealing with Garou as Wraiths and Risen which is that they reek of death and are intuitively a subversion of natural order.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T14:41:03-07:00","id":1539053,"title":"","content":"Sin-Eaters would probably be better off than Risen because they're not in a bad situation courtesy of their Shadow, but note that ghosts aren't ever noted as being inherently bad to Garou, and in particular, not to Silent Striders or members of the Ivory Priesthood.<br /> <br /> Prometheans are existential threats to their surroundings. There's no principle at work here if the Garou kill them; they'd do it for the same reason that they kill Mages that trespass into caerns.<br /> <br /> Renegades are probably considered better than vampires, at a basement. Manticores might even end up as Gorgons for a double-shot of power, and the Red Talons and/or Ratkin would probably be really interested in helping them get their revenge.<br /> <br /> I would never use Beast at all. I have a grudge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T15:59:20-07:00","id":1539061,"title":"","content":"The Lost and the Demons from Descent are both distinct enough from their &quot;counterparts&quot; as to deserve their own entries in this exercise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-22T17:07:50-07:00","id":1539067,"title":"","content":"Agree with what monteparnas has said above re: Lost &amp; Descent.<br /> <br /> Also it\u2019s been a while since I\u2019ve really gotten into the groove of the chronicles splat so this might be a bit rusty, but starting from the top-down:<br /> <br /> <b>Prometheans</b><br /> One thing I like about the Created is that you can imagine them actually co-existing in the WoD fairly easily, at least imo. They fill in the classic Frankenstein\u2019s Monster slot from the Universal Horror catalogue, further expanding room for that archetype from what we get re: Mage and its examples of creating artificial humanoids who, if left alone, could probably function like bygones or something similar.<br /> <br /> That said, I agree that for Gaians the alien nature and environmental effect of the Created would be the biggest red flag. Seeker types like theurges or Bastet may want to pin down the precise nature of their origins and the Pilgrimage and so forth but I do think the orthodox Gaian reaction would be that the Created are outside of the natural order and, worse still, constitute an existential threat to it (although I think you could have a lively debate over which Triatic influence they would be most associated with). Regardless I see the Gaian line being a tendency towards elimination of them as a threat, or, bare minimum, attempting to harass and drive them away into certain barren zones where they represent a minimum threat.<br /> <br /> <b>Lost</b><br /> To a certain extent I can see the Lost being treated similarly to spirit-ridden (like Fomori or Drones) or Changelings; clearly having been altered by contact with a bizarre set of alien spiritual beings, although not inherently a threat to the Gaian order. I imagine that most Gaians would ignore most Courts under normal circumstances, except perhaps Corax or Bastet who are too curious for their own good. While the Gaians may recognize the Lost as simply being a strange type of spirit-ridden, I imagine they\u2019d still avoid Contracts with them, preferring their more orthodox spiritual partners.<br /> <br /> The biggest concern I can see would be the potential for kinfolk to become Lost. This I see as being where the most intervention would be possible, although not necessarily to the Lost\u2019s benefit. Similar to the mixed reactions possible for Changeling or Mage kinfolk really, except probably not as benevolent as the Dreaming Changelings probably seem more approachable (particularly with the Fianna in mind) then the alien spirits of the Hedge. Lost Kinfolk going into hiding to avoid mercy killings wouldn\u2019t be off the table imo. <br /> <br /> <b>Sin-Eaters</b><br /> The Sin-Eaters would be interesting imo. They\u2019re not inherently a threat to the constitution of the natural world and Gaians are not necessarily border patrol in the same way that the Forsaken are. Most Gaians may even see contemporary humans gaining a better relationship with the spirits of the dead as a positive development and, to that extent, might even consider offering some amount of support and aid to the Sin-Eaters.<br /> <br /> However, this is also somewhat dependent on what a krewe and their spiritual counterparts are getting up to. As many Gaians would probably point out, there\u2019s a big difference in Gaians receiving guidance from their also Gaian ancestors and Weaver/Wyrm-tainted humans taking their cues from dead humans who were probably no more enlightened then their contemporaries. While many ghosts and Sin-Eaters would probably be benign, others may draw Gaian intervention. Of course, groups like the Silent Striders would probably have a more complex and proactive view, seeing as how much of their activity does include ensuring their views on correct cthonic relations are upheld and they may clash with Sin-Eaters whose philosophies differed from their own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-24T11:28:00-07:00","id":1539151,"title":"","content":"Continuing from my previous section <br /> (note: I have yet to read Deviant)<br /> <br /> <b>Unchained</b><br /> The Unchained of Descent are interesting to me because one has to ask what role the God-Machine would have in the WoD. While one could posit it as an almost eldritch horror of inscrutable origin, which has some merit, I\u2019m going to lean into its Infrastructure-building aspect and imagine it as a sort of curious type of tainted Weaver incarna (at least from a Gaian\u2019s perspective), whose goal is to preserve the World of Darkness by whatever means possible, but without addressing the causes of underlying rot, thus making it a sickly conservator performing unseemly acts merely to prolong a collapse into greater sickness.<br /> <br /> The principal issue for Gaians would likely be to distinguish Angels from Demons. If Gaians become aware of either, it would probably be Angels that appear to defend Infrastructure from Gaians, who would likely be seeking to monkey-wrench it. This may cause them to attack Demons without realizing that the Unchained operate differently then Angels. Even those who become aware of the distinction are still probably wary of them, the same way they\u2019d be wary of a Wyrm jaggling that told them it was against the larger Wyrm-as-such for its own reasons and thus was willing to help them. Some groups, such as the tribes of the Shadow Lords or Ghost Council (Older Brother), might be willing to accept that but many others would not. I imagine a similar situation where Bone Gnawers, Glasswalkers, Ananasi, &amp; some Ratkin consent to work with the Demons while other Gaians ignore them at best of attack them the same as Angels or GM Infrastructure. <br /> <br /> <b>Primordial</b><br /> The Primordials are tapped into something like the human collective unconscious and would probably be drawn to the Gaians, particularly those who are able to cause the Delirium. For their part I imagine many Gaians would, again, view the Primordials as a strange form of spirit-ridden, one\u2019s particularly connected with the psychic maelstrom and cthonic subconscious of a region. Some mystery seekers like the Bastet, Ghost Council, or Silent Striders may find them particularly intriguing but I imagine many others finding attempts by Beasts to approach them as undesirable at best and aggressive at worst.<br /> <br /> The big issue I think would be that Beasts may not draw a distinction between Black Spiral Dancers and Gaians, the former who might be particularly hospitable to them. If Gaians discovered Beasts offered aid or comfort to their enemies, such as the Wyrm-fallen BSD or the like, they would probably classify Beasts as wyrm-tainted and target them for destruction as enemies, barring perhaps the odd Stargazer who cares to puzzle out the nuances and peculiarities of the Primordial\u2019s drives.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}